<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> In This Region, They Have a Custom Of...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present on a personally significant topic. Interactive: Engage in discussions about various real-world topics, asking and responding to questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present about a personally significant tradition, directly addressing the specific standard (e.g., "My family celebrates Christmas with special food.").
Can engage in discussions about cultural traditions by asking and responding to questions and using follow-up prompts (e.g., "What is your favorite tradition?" "I like Fiestas Patrias because of the parades." "What do you like most about the parade?").
Can ask and respond to questions about regional festivals by providing relevant details (e.g., "When do people celebrate the Manito Ocuero Festival?" "We celebrate it in August.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a vivid image of a popular Panamanian celebration (e.g., Pollera Festival, Fiestas Patrias parade). Students work in pairs to brainstorm two or three words related to the image. The teacher writes these words on the board, eliciting vocabulary like "parade," "celebration," "dance," "traditional," "cultural," "festive," "gather." Students share their ideas with the class. Example prompt: "What do you see in this picture? What kind of event is it?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models phrases for describing traditions and expressing preferences, using the image and some of the brainstormed words. For example: "This is a <b>traditional celebration</b>. People <b>gather</b> and <b>dance</b>. I like this <b>festival</b> because it's very <b>festive</b>. My family <b>celebrates</b> Fiestas Patrias with a big <b>parade</b>. We really <b>enjoy</b> marching <b>together</b>." The teacher explicitly models word stress for "mask," "gather," "cultural," "celebration" and highlights the sounds of irregular present tense verbs ending in -s/-es/-ies like "decorates" /s/, "gathers" /z/, and "wishes" /ɪz/ by saying them clearly and having students repeat. Example: "She <b>decorates</b> her home. He <b>gathers</b> with friends. Everyone <b>wishes</b> each other well."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and grammar.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'parade' a type of food or a public procession?" (Expected answer: A public procession.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I am excited about <b>celebrating</b>,' am I talking about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound does 'celebrates' make at the end?" (Expected answer: /s/). "What about 'enjoys'?" (Expected answer: /z/).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio clip or a dialogue transcript about "The Manito Ocueño Festival" or "Diablicos Sucios" tradition. This material will be short, authentic, and descriptive, focusing on how people celebrate, what they wear, and when it happens. (e.g., "The Manito Ocueño Festival is a cultural celebration in August. People gather to dance traditional dances and enjoy local cuisine. It's a custom that showcases our heritage.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in class discussions about regional and cultural celebrations, responding to questions about traditions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen/read for gist and specific details.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main topic of this dialogue/audio?" (Expected: A Panamanian festival/tradition).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b>
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The festival is celebrated in July." (False - if it's in August)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People wear traditional masks and costumes." (True - depending on the text)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This tradition shows the local heritage." (True)</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match key vocabulary (e.g., "cuisine," "heritage," "ritual") from the text to simple definitions or images.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice describing celebrations using Present simple and Past simple, gerunds after prepositions, and Present perfect. They use vocabulary like "tradition," "custom," "celebration," "parade," "dance," "enjoy," "gather," "traditional," "cultural." Focus on accurate pronunciation of word stress and third-person singular verb endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion (Individual to Pair Work):</b> The teacher provides sentences with gaps. Students complete them using the target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Panama ______ (celebrate) Fiestas Patrias on November 3rd and 4th." (<i>celebrates</i>)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My grandmother told me about the old ______ (custom) of ______ (prepare) special food for harvest." (<i>custom, preparing</i>)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People ______ (participate) in parades for many years. They ______ (do) that since independence." (<i>have participated, have done</i>)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then orally check their answers with a partner, practicing correct pronunciation.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Question-and-Answer Chains:</b> The teacher provides a set of questions and prompts. Students, in pairs, ask and answer, focusing on using the target structures.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What traditions are important in your community?" (Student A asks) "My community <b>observes</b> the 'Festival de la Mejorana' in September. It <b>is</b> a very old <b>custom</b>." (Student B responds, practicing /z/ in 'observes').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do people do during this <b>celebration</b>?" (Student A asks, practicing word stress on 'celebration'). "They <b>gather</b> to <b>dance</b> and <b>enjoy traditional cuisine</b>." (Student B responds, practicing /z/ in 'gathers').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Have you ever <b>participated</b> in a <b>parade</b>?" (Student A asks). "Yes, I <b>have participated</b> in the Fiestas Patrias <b>parade</b>. I was excited about <b>marching</b> with my school band." (Student B responds, using present perfect and gerund, and word stress on 'parade').</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: Describing Celebrations:</b> Students get picture cards of different local traditions (e.g., Carnaval, Corpus Christi, Fiestas Patrias). In pairs, one student picks a card and describes the celebration to their partner, who then asks two follow-up questions. They must use at least two of the target grammar structures (present/past simple, gerund, present perfect) and three vocabulary words (e.g., "tradition," "dance," "festive," "mask," "fireworks"). The emphasis is on speaking accurately and asking/responding to questions.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Pair Interview and Presentation (role-play / collaborative interaction).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each student chooses a personally significant tradition or a regional custom they know well (e.g., a family Christmas, a local festival, a national day).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A interviews Student B about their chosen tradition, asking questions such as: "What is your favorite tradition or custom?" "When do people celebrate it?" "What do people traditionally do?" "What is special about this celebration?" "Have you ever participated in a key ritual or ceremony?" "What do you like most about it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B responds, providing details using the target grammar (present simple for description, past simple for specific events, present perfect for experiences, gerunds after prepositions) and vocabulary (e.g., "celebration," "cuisine," "dance," "parade," "heritage," "festive," "gather"). They must also express their preferences (e.g., "My favorite tradition is... because...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After 3-4 minutes, they switch roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each pair briefly (1-2 minutes) presents to another pair or the class a summary of their partner's tradition, using phrases like "My partner's favorite tradition is... In this region, they have a custom of... They have participated in... He/She is excited about celebrating..." The focus is on fluent, communicative exchange and presenting information about a tradition.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the pair interview and the summary presentation described in Stage 4. The teacher circulates, observing students' speaking. Students also use a simple peer assessment checklist to evaluate their partner's performance during the interview, focusing on clarity, use of vocabulary, and question-asking/responding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist/Peer Assessment):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, including word stress and correct third-person singular verb endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Effectively uses words like "tradition," "custom," "celebration," "parade," "cultural," "festive," "gather," "enjoy."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Communicates ideas about traditions clearly and in a logical order.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Engagement in discussion:</b> Asks and responds to questions effectively, uses follow-up prompts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Incorporates present simple, past simple, gerunds after prepositions, and present perfect correctly.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today was __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel confident describing __________ in English."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of talking about traditions did I find easy today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was challenging, and how can I improve my speaking about celebrations?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities worked well, with most students engaging actively in describing traditions and asking questions. The focus on specific pronunciation points like third-person singular endings helped improve intelligibility. Some students struggled with consistently using the present perfect tense for experiences. Next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will reinforce the vocabulary and grammar by having students write descriptive paragraphs about traditions. I will plan to provide sentence starters and models to support their written output and ensure they transfer the oral practice into written form, especially with present perfect and gerunds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to orally describe traditions. The next lesson will focus on writing compositions about these same traditions, allowing students to consolidate the vocabulary and grammar learned and practiced today into coherent written paragraphs. They will write about "My Favorite Local Tradition" or "A Custom In This Region," using the descriptions and ideas generated today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>